/* ---- VOCAB DATA ---- */
const vocab = {
  federalism: { title: 'Federalism', def: 'A system where power is divided between a central (federal) government and regional governments (states, provinces, Länder), each with their own guaranteed powers that neither can simply abolish.', examples: 'USA: states have their own criminal law, taxes, education systems. Germany: Länder have substantial powers and a chamber (Bundesrat) in federal government. Contrast with unitary states (France, UK) where regions exist by permission of the centre.', why: 'Federalism suits large, diverse countries — it allows regions to adapt policy to local needs. The downside: complexity, potential inconsistency (laws differ by state), and gridlock when federal and state governments conflict.' },
  separation: { title: 'Separation of powers', def: 'Dividing governmental power among three branches: executive (government/president — makes decisions), legislative (parliament — makes laws), and judicial (courts — interprets laws). Each branch checks the others.', examples: 'USA: the clearest separation — Congress, President, and Supreme Court are fully independent. UK: less strict — the Prime Minister leads both the executive and commands parliament. EU: complex mix of Commission (executive), Parliament, and Council.', why: 'Prevents concentration of power. If one person or party controls all three branches, there are no checks on abuse. The Founders of the USA designed the separation specifically because they feared tyranny.' },
  constitution: { title: 'Constitution', def: 'A set of fundamental rules defining how a state is organised, what powers the government has, and what rights citizens hold. Acts as a higher law that ordinary legislation cannot override.', examples: 'Written: USA (1787, the world\'s oldest still-operating), Germany, France, India. Unwritten: UK — no single document; conventions, laws, and precedents function together as a constitution. Israel has no formal constitution; Basic Laws serve the function.', why: 'Constitutional courts (like the German Federal Constitutional Court or US Supreme Court) can strike down laws that violate the constitution. This protects minorities against majority tyranny but raises the question: who elected the judges?' },
  referendum: { title: 'Referendum / plebiscite', def: 'A direct vote in which the electorate votes on a specific question rather than for a party or candidate. Used for major constitutional decisions, policy questions, or independence votes.', examples: 'Brexit (UK 2016): 52% voted Leave. Scottish independence (2014): 55% voted No. Swiss direct democracy: up to 4 national referendums per year on any topic. Ireland uses referendums for constitutional changes (abortion, same-sex marriage).', why: 'Referendums bypass representative institutions, giving citizens direct say. Critics: complex policy reduced to yes/no; susceptible to misinformation; simple majority can harm minorities; politicians use them to escape responsibility for difficult choices.' },
  gerrymander: { title: 'Gerrymandering', def: 'Drawing electoral district boundaries to favour one party over another. By packing your opponents into a few districts (where they win big but "waste" votes) or cracking their voters across many districts (so they never reach a majority), you can win more seats than your vote share deserves.', examples: 'Named after Governor Elbridge Gerry of Massachusetts, who in 1812 signed a district shaped like a salamander into law. Widespread in the USA where state legislatures draw their own districts. Less possible in proportional systems or where independent commissions draw boundaries (UK, Germany).', why: 'Can make elections dramatically unrepresentative. A party winning 55% of votes might win 70% of seats due to gerrymandering. Increasingly algorithmic — computers optimise district shapes to maximise partisan advantage.' },
  coalition: { title: 'Coalition government', def: 'When no single party wins enough seats to govern alone, multiple parties form a coalition, sharing cabinet posts and agreeing on a joint programme. Coalition agreements are often detailed documents specifying shared policies.', examples: 'Germany has been governed by coalitions for almost its entire post-war history — CDU/SPD "grand coalitions," CDU/FDP, SPD/Greens/FDP ("traffic light"). Italy averages a new government every ~15 months. The Netherlands often takes months to form a coalition after elections.', why: 'Coalitions force compromise and can represent more voters. Downsides: instability (coalition partners can withdraw support), voters can\'t predict who will govern with whom, and small parties can hold disproportionate power as "kingmakers."' },
  sovereignty: { title: 'Sovereignty', def: 'The supreme authority of a state to govern itself without external interference. A sovereign state makes its own laws, controls its territory, and is not subject to any higher legal authority.', examples: 'EU membership involves pooling sovereignty — members accept EU law as superior to national law in agreed areas. Brexit was partly about reclaiming sovereignty from EU institutions. International law and treaties are voluntary — states accept them but can (in theory) withdraw. The UN cannot legally enforce decisions against a member\'s will without using force.', why: 'Sovereignty is increasingly contested in practice: international trade agreements limit economic sovereignty, human rights law limits what states can do to their own citizens, and military and economic interdependence makes true sovereignty theoretical for all but the most powerful states.' },
  checks: { title: 'Checks and balances', def: 'Mechanisms that prevent any one branch of government or political actor from accumulating unchecked power. Each branch has tools to limit the others: legislatures can impeach executives, courts can strike down laws, executives can veto legislation.', examples: 'USA: the president can veto Congress; Congress can override the veto with 2/3 majority; the Supreme Court can strike down both. Germany: the Bundesrat (representing Länder) must approve laws affecting state powers. UK: Parliament can pass a vote of no confidence removing the government.', why: 'Without checks and balances, democracy becomes "elective dictatorship" — whoever wins an election can do whatever they want. Hungary is a recent case of what happens when checks are systematically dismantled: Orbán won a supermajority, changed the electoral law, packed the courts, and effectively removed institutional constraints on his government.' }
};

