    centrist: '"A large demonstration was held in the capital. Organisers claimed 50,000 attendees; police estimated 20,000. Some disruption was reported."',
    lesson: 'Same event. Left framing: citizens heroically speaking out. Right framing: selfish disruption. Centrist: contested numbers, neutral verbs. Notice how the choice of "activists" vs "citizens" and "disruption" vs "demand" signals editorial position without stating it directly.'
  },
  budget: {
    title: 'Government announces 15% reduction in housing benefit',
    left: '"Government slashes housing benefit, pushing thousands of vulnerable families closer to homelessness."',
    right: '"Government reforms welfare spending to encourage work and sustainability, saving £2bn for frontline services."',
    centrist: '"The government has announced a 15% reduction in housing benefit, affecting an estimated 400,000 claimants. Ministers say the savings will fund other priorities; critics warn of increased homelessness."',
    lesson: '"Slashes" vs "reforms" — one word does enormous emotional work. The left framing humanises ("families," "homelessness"); the right frames around abstract virtues (work, sustainability) and a benefit (frontline services). The centrist gives numbers and attributes views to named parties rather than editorialising.'
  },
  migration: {
    title: 'Record number of asylum seekers arrive',
    left: '"Record numbers of asylum seekers arrive fleeing war and persecution, as humanitarian crisis deepens and government urged to expand safe routes."',
    right: '"Record illegal immigration crisis as border controls overwhelmed — government under pressure to act."',
    centrist: '"A record 120,000 asylum seekers arrived in the first half of the year, according to official figures. The government faces pressure from multiple directions on its response."',
    lesson: '"Asylum seekers fleeing persecution" vs "illegal immigration" — legally, asylum seekers are not illegal immigrants (they have the right to apply). But "illegal" primes the audience to view the situation as criminal. Note how the right framing frames the government as victim ("overwhelmed") while the left frames it as morally responsible.'
  },
  strike: {
    title: 'Train workers strike for the third consecutive week',
    left: '"Train workers maintain strike action as dispute over real-terms pay cuts continues, with union warning that conditions have deteriorated for years."',
    right: '"Commuters held hostage for third week as militant unions cripple rail network and cost economy millions."',
    centrist: '"Rail workers are in their third week of strike action over a pay dispute. Disruption has affected hundreds of thousands of commuters. Talks between the union and management broke down last week."',
    lesson: '"Held hostage" and "militant" are not neutral news words — they are rhetoric. "Cripple" frames union action as illegitimate attack. The left framing uses "maintain" (principled continuation) and "real-terms pay cuts" (framing the workers as defending ground, not gaining). The centrist sticks to verifiable facts and avoids attributing motive.'
  },
  crime: {
    title: 'Government releases annual crime statistics',
    left: '"Poverty-linked crime rises as austerity bites: experts link surge in theft and burglary to decade of welfare cuts and rising inequality."',
    right: '"Law and order crisis: crime rises for fifth year as soft sentencing fails to deter criminals and police numbers remain below 2010 levels."',
    centrist: '"Recorded crime rose 8% last year, driven largely by increases in theft. The government and opposition disagree on the causes and appropriate response."',
    lesson: 'Both partisan framings contain facts but select the cause that fits their worldview: left (structural causes — poverty, austerity), right (individual causes — inadequate punishment, police numbers). Both omit facts that complicate their narrative. Notice how "experts say" (left) and "crisis" (right) are rhetorical moves — one appeals to authority, the other to emergency.'
  }
};

